<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrea Palladio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1508–<a href="/years/1580">1580</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrea Palladio was an architect of the Venetian Republic, active mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicenza">Vicenza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. His villas on the Venetian mainland, his palaces in Vicenza and his Venetian churches, San Giorgio Maggiore and the Redentore, applied the forms of ancient Roman architecture with great clarity. His treatise The Four Books of Architecture (1570) spread his style across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, especially to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrea-palladio.jpg" alt="Andrea Palladio" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alessandro Maganza · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrea_Palladio2.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
